course: dictionary
lesson: 5047
title: Words about people in stories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An innkeeper is a person who owns or runs an inn, a small hotel or pub where travellers can eat, drink and sleep. Read the clues around the word. «The kind innkeeper» tells you the innkeeper is a person, and the word «kind» tells you how they treat travellers.
explain: Look inside the word. Innkeeper is made of two smaller words, inn and keeper. A keeper looks after something, so an innkeeper looks after an inn. Ask yourself what would fit, then check your meaning in the sentence.
explain: In the Christmas story, «no room at the inn» means every bed is full, so the innkeeper cannot take Mary and Joseph in. In the Good Samaritan story, a Samaritan «paid an innkeeper to look after» a hurt man. The context shows the innkeeper cares for travellers who need a bed and help.
fact: Innkeeper is a noun. One is an innkeeper, and more than one is innkeepers.
fact: An inn is a small hotel or pub where travellers can eat, drink and sleep.
fact: «The kind innkeeper» shows a helpful person who looks after travellers.
fact: «No room at the inn» means there is no free bed for the travellers.
fact: In the Good Samaritan story, a Samaritan «paid an innkeeper to look after» a hurt man.
exercise: «The kind innkeeper gave the tired travellers a warm meal and a bed.» What is an innkeeper? | A horse that pulls a cart | A traveller who walks a very long way | *A person who runs an inn where travellers eat and sleep | A type of soup
exercise: Ella reads «no room at the inn». What does it mean? | The inn has too many rooms to count | *There is no free bed for the travellers | The inn is cold and empty | The travellers have forgotten their shoes
exercise: Innkeeper is two smaller words joined together. Which of these is a word inside innkeeper that names a small hotel or pub? | keep | kind | *inn | keeper
test: «The Samaritan paid an innkeeper to look after the hurt man.» What does an innkeeper do? | Builds new castles | Sells horses at the market | Hurts travellers on the road | *Looks after travellers who need care and a bed
test: In the Christmas story, why does the innkeeper send Mary and Joseph away? | They have no money to pay for a cup of tea | The innkeeper is asleep | They are too noisy | *There is no room left for them at the inn
exam: Which sentence uses innkeeper correctly? | *The innkeeper welcomed the tired travellers and showed them to their room | The innkeeper was a red bird that sang at dawn | Innkeeper is a sort of soup for winter | Innkeeper means a long, thin pencil
